course: everyday
lesson: 488
title: The electric meter, the food bank and «we can't afford it»
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Middlesbrough the telly dies right in the middle of Lorcan's cartoons, and the lights go with it. «Meter's run out, pet, I'll top up tomorrow,» says Mum («pet» is a North East word for love or dear). Some homes have a meter you pay for in advance, like a pay-as-you-go phone: when the money on it runs out, the electricity stops. They eat tea by torchlight, which is an adventure until Lorcan gets cold. Many families face this, it's nobody's fault, and there is emergency credit, and charities and the council can help if it keeps happening.
explain: In Toronto, Priyanka's mom has lost her job, so they go to a food bank, a place that gives free food to families who need it. In the line Priyanka sees a boy from her class. They look at each other and both look away. She's embarrassed, and also weirdly relieved she's not the only one. Lots of families use food banks, even families where the grown-ups work. It's help from the community, not a shame, and the kind thing is never to tell others who you saw there.
explain: Grown-ups say money things in all sorts of ways. Rosie's dad in Bristol says, «We can't afford it, my lover» («my lover» is a friendly West Country way of saying dear), and Rosie yells, «We can NEVER afford anything!» and slams the door. «We can't afford it» means there isn't enough left after rent, food and bills; it isn't a punishment and it isn't about love. In Atlanta, Tyrone's mom says, «We fixin' to tighten belts. No more takeout.» («Fixin' to» means about to; «tighten belts» means spend less.) «Are we poor?» «We're careful.» Tyrone helps Grandma make cornbread and feels useful.
explain: Children often hear bits of money talk and imagine the worst. In Edinburgh, Zofia hears «the rent» and «maybe moving» through the wall and can't sleep, worrying about a new school; asking her parents straight is less scary than guessing. In Hull, the money Marley's mum gets from the government comes late, the fridge is nearly empty, and Mum makes «Marley's Magic Soup» out of everything left. He knows what it means and plays along and eats two bowls, which is kind. If a child is often hungry, telling a teacher helps, because schools can help quietly.
explain: Sometimes the hard bit is home being empty or cold. In Swansea, Osian's mum has two jobs, café by day and cleaning offices at night, and leaves notes: «Love you, cariad, back at 10» («cariad» is Welsh for love). He makes his own toast and puts himself to bed; children shouldn't be alone at night for long, so if he's scared, he should tell Mum or a teacher, and there may be help like an after-school club. In Limerick, Aoife's mam says, «We don't need charity,» about the winter coat scheme, but Aoife's zip is broken; she can tell her teacher, who can talk to Mam gently.
explain: Children notice even when parents try to hide it. In London, Leon finds a £5 note and thinks of sweets, then of Dad counting coins at the till. He gives it to Dad, who laughs, hugs him and says, «Spend a pound on sweets, eh.» Wanting to help is kind, but fixing money problems is a grown-up's job. In Norfolk, a bad harvest means one small present each for Maisie-Jo's family this Christmas. «It's the weather, girl, not anybody's fault,» says Dad, and she makes everyone homemade cards.
fact: A prepayment meter is paid in advance; when the money on it runs out, the electricity or gas stops until it is topped up.
fact: Prepayment meters often have emergency credit, and councils and charities can help families who run out often.
fact: A food bank gives free food to families who need it; many families use them, including families where the grown-ups work.
fact: If you see someone you know at a food bank, the kind thing is to keep it private.
fact: «We can't afford it» means there isn't enough money left after rent, food and bills; it is not a punishment and not about love.
fact: «Tightening belts» means spending less for a while; families do it when money is tight, and it doesn't mean danger.
fact: Children often hear bits of grown-up money talk and imagine the worst; asking a parent is usually less scary than guessing.
fact: Many parents work two jobs to pay the bills; children shouldn't be left alone at night for long, and a child who is scared or alone a lot should tell a parent or teacher.
fact: Some parents find it hard to accept help because of pride, but help schemes exist so children stay warm and fed.
fact: A child who is often hungry or cold can tell a teacher; schools can help quietly.
fact: Some families' money depends on things nobody controls, like the weather at harvest; a bad year is not anybody's fault.
fact: It's kind for a child to want to help, but sorting out money problems is a grown-up's job.
fact: Words in this lesson: «pet» (North East England) and «my lover» (West Country) mean dear, «cariad» is Welsh for love, «fixin' to» (US South) means about to, «skint» means having no money.
exercise: Why did Lorcan's lights and telly go off? | The money on the meter ran out | There was a big storm outside | Mum forgot to pay the TV licence | Lorcan pulled the plug by mistake
exercise: How does a prepayment meter work? | You pay first; no money, no power | You pay at the end of the year for all you used | It's free, and the council pays for everybody | It runs on the coins in a piggy bank at home
exercise: What did Mum say she'd do about the meter? | Top up tomorrow | Move house | Call the police | Buy a new telly
exercise: «Meter's run out, pet.» Who is «pet»? | Lorcan, it means dear | The family cat | The meter man | Lorcan's baby sister only
exercise: What is a food bank? | Free food for families who need it | A bank where you can pay with tins and packets | A shop that only sells food in very big boxes | A safe where rich people keep their posh food
exercise: Priyanka and the boy from class looked away from each other. What's the kind thing to do next? | Keep it to herself | Tell the whole class at break time | Ask him loudly why he was there | Post about it on the class chat
exercise: «We can't afford it, my lover.» What does Dad mean? | There isn't enough money left after the bills | He doesn't love Rosie enough to buy it | Rosie is being punished for being cheeky | He'll buy it if she shouts a bit louder
exercise: «We fixin' to tighten belts.» What is Tyrone's mom saying? | We're about to spend less | We're buying new belts for school | We're going on a diet | We're fixing the car
exercise: What did Tyrone do that helped him feel better? | Helped Grandma make cornbread | Hid in his room all night | Ordered a takeout in secret | Asked a neighbour for money
exercise: Zofia heard «rent» and «maybe moving» and can't sleep. What's the best move? | Ask her parents | Pack her bag to be ready | Keep worrying and say nothing | Tell her friends she's leaving
exercise: Why does Marley's mum call it «Marley's Magic Soup»? | To turn a hard time into a game for him | Because she's learning magic tricks | Because Marley invented the recipe | Because it's Marley's birthday today
exercise: Osian's mum leaves notes: «back at 10». Why is she out? | She works a second job cleaning offices | She's out with friends every night | She has forgotten about him at home | She's at the café from 6 till midnight
exercise: What can Osian do if he's often alone and scared at night? | Tell Mum or a teacher | Go out to find Mum | Open the door to anyone | Keep it a secret
exercise: «We don't need charity,» says Aoife's mam. What's going on? | Pride makes it hard for Mam to accept help | Mam has already bought Aoife a new coat | The coat scheme is only for boys this year | Mam thinks Aoife's coat is still brand new
exercise: What did Leon do with the £5 he found? | Gave it to Dad | Bought sweets | Hid it in a sock | Lost it again
exercise: Whose fault was the bad harvest at Maisie-Jo's? | Nobody's, it was the weather | Maisie-Jo's, for wanting presents | Dad's, for planting the wrong seeds | The neighbours', for taking the rain
exercise: What did Maisie-Jo make for everyone that Christmas? | Homemade cards | A big chocolate cake | A snowman | Paper hats
test: «only lazy ppl go to food banks innit». Right? | No, lots of families use them, even working ones | Yes, if they worked they'd never be hungry | Yes, food banks only let in lazy people | No, food banks are only for the very rich
test: The meter runs out and Lorcan's little cousin yells, «we're gonna be in the dark FOREVER». What's true? | They'll be in the dark only until it's topped up | Once a meter runs out it can never be refilled | The power company takes the whole house away | Electricity only comes back on the next birthday
test: Rosie: «we can NEVER afford anything, you don't even care!» What's more likely true? | Dad cares, there just isn't enough money | Dad has money but wants to punish her | Dad would rather buy himself a new phone | Dad will change his mind if she slams doors
test: Rosie slammed the door, then heard Dad sigh in the kitchen. What could she do to fix it? | Say sorry, it's OK to have been cross | Slam it again so he knows she means it | Never ask for anything ever again | Ask Gran to buy the phone in secret
test: What would probably happen if Zofia never asked and kept guessing? | She'd keep worrying, maybe about things that aren't true | She'd find out everything by reading her mum's mind | Her parents would move the family that same night | The rent would go back down if she stayed quiet
test: In a TV show, a kid secretly sells his bike to pay his family's rent and saves the day. What's more real? | Rent is a grown-up's job, and kids can't fix it | Every kid can pay the rent by selling toys | Landlords only accept money from children | Selling one bike pays rent for a whole year
test: Marley: «Mum's soup again lol, it's well nice tho». He's hungry most days at school. What should happen? | He tells a teacher | He takes food from other kids' lunchboxes | He keeps it secret so Mum isn't embarrassed | He stops eating breakfast to make it last
test: Aoife's coat zip is broken and Mam won't sign up for the coat scheme. Who could gently help? | Her teacher, by talking to Mam | Nobody, she has to stay cold | Her friends, by laughing at Mam | The coat shop, by giving one away
test: Leon found £5. Dad says, «Spend a pound on sweets.» How much goes back to the family money? | £4 | £5 | £1 | £6
test: Tyrone's mom: «no takeout for a month». They used to get takeout every Friday at $20 a time. A month has 4 Fridays. How much do they save? | $80 | $20 | $40 | $24
test: Maisie-Jo: «it's cos I asked for too much last year, isn't it». What does Dad's «It's the weather, girl» tell her? | It's not her fault, or anybody's | She asked for too much last year | Dad blames her for the harvest | The weather is angry with them
test: Osian's mum works till 10. His friend says, «u can do what u want, lucky». What's more true? | Being alone a lot is hard, and grown-ups should know | Being alone every night is just fun and nothing else | Kids are meant to look after themselves at night | He should invite mates round while Mum's at work
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a prepayment meter? | A device paid in advance, the supply ceasing when the credit is exhausted | A device whose charges are settled once a year, in arrears, by the household | A device whose supply is provided gratuitously by the local council | A device which generates electricity independently of any supplier
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the users of food banks. | Many families, including working ones, use them | They are used solely by persons unwilling to work | Their use is restricted to persons of great wealth | They are used only during national emergencies
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the parental statement «we cannot afford it». | Insufficient money remains after rent, food and bills | The child is being punished for prior misconduct | The parent's affection for the child has diminished | The item is prohibited by law for persons of that age
exam: Given that a child is frequently alone and frightened in the evenings while a parent works, ascertain the most appropriate course. | To inform the parent or a teacher | To leave the house in search of the parent | To admit any visitor who knocks | To conceal the matter from all adults
